From d296250894f32d2e7e89e094abc67497fe3164b0 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mika Tammi <mikatammi@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 15:17:50 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] rts: don't compile XXH3 on 32-bit platforms

hashBuffer only uses XXH3 when WORD_SIZE_IN_BITS == 64 and XXH32
otherwise, so XXH3 is dead code on 32-bit platforms.

On wasm32 it is also harmful: with -msimd128, xxhash 0.8.3 includes
<arm_neon.h> whenever __has_include(<arm_neon.h>) succeeds, expecting
SIMDe's NEON emulation as provided by Emscripten. A clang that is built
with ARM targets ships its own arm_neon.h in the resource directory,
which errors out for wasm:

    rts/xxhash.h:3852: arm_neon.h:28:2: error: "<arm_neon.h> is intended only for ARM and AArch64 targets"

Define XXH_NO_XXH3 when the word size isn't 64 bits.

Assisted-by: Claude Code (claude-opus-5-5)
---
 rts/Hash.c | 6 ++++++
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+)

diff --git a/rts/Hash.c b/rts/Hash.c
index 8b449f2..8900fbe 100644
--- a/rts/Hash.c
+++ b/rts/Hash.c
@@ -14,6 +14,12 @@
 #include "Hash.h"
 #include "RtsUtils.h"
 
+#if WORD_SIZE_IN_BITS != 64
+/* XXH3 is only used on 64-bit platforms (see hashBuffer). Leaving it out
+ * also keeps xxhash from including <arm_neon.h> for wasm SIMD128 (meant for
+ * SIMDe), which fails with clangs that ship ARM headers. */
+#define XXH_NO_XXH3
+#endif
 #define XXH_INLINE_ALL
 
 #include "xxhash.h"
